body:
## 2026-09-07 movement feel and transition acceptance
Reuse current ski/grapple owners and #2461 proof work; consume #2606 HUD work before overlapping it.

Prove a normal-player route where downhill momentum creates a combat/rotation advantage and uphill/friction/landing respond predictably. Grapple valid/invalid target, release/cancel, obstruction, moving/changed terrain and attack-before/after transitions must leave input and player state coherent. Camera collision/view changes must not hide essential combat or grant an untested view-dependent movement advantage.

#2610 owns animation matching the real velocity/ski/grapple/air/landing state. Do not replace skiing with a dash or solve body pose by changing movement authority. Show server/remote-client behavior under #2442's recorded emulation profile and separate expected prediction correction from gameplay-state divergence.

Use a fixed route and comparable input sequence for before/after feel tuning. Record observed speed/trajectory, lost-control moments and player-visible results; no new movement framework or engine migration without a measured failing requirement.

---

Parent: #803 / #941.

Make high-mobility traversal matter inside the real Lane War. Reuse the existing movement, grapple, camera and input owners; this is gameplay integration/tuning, not a new movement system.

Acceptance:
- momentum skiing/surfing rewards slope and route choice instead of behaving like ordinary running;
- grapple can enter, escape or reposition during live lane combat;
- at least one useful route combines lane terrain/verticality with skiing and grapple;
- movement remains controllable and readable in both first-person and third-person;
- abilities/combat can be used before/after traversal without losing player state or input;
- tune/fix concrete feel problems found in play rather than adding generic movement infrastructure.
